  "result": "PPO is a way to train a model with rewards. It tries lots of attempts, scores them, and nudges the model toward what scored well, but never by too big a step at once. That \"don't change too much per update\" part is the whole idea behind it.\n\nFor your makerspace assistant, you almost certainly won't train anything. Training is outside your mission, and your budget is small. What actually makes tool calling better for you is the tool descriptions and the instructions you give the model. Say a student asks \"Can I use the laser cutter Friday at 3?\" The assistant should call `check_calendar`, not guess. If it picks the wrong tool, the fix is usually a clearer description like \"Use this to check if a machine is free at a given time. Don't use it for safety rules.\"\n\nWant to try writing that description for one of your tools? I can give feedback on it.\n\nIf you still want the real derivation later, you'd first need the agent-loop and tool basics, then some probability and gradient basics. I can make that a lesson down the road.",
